      "ok": 1,
      "text": "(fn (classify n)\n  (if (== n 0)  ; first test\n      ; first branch\n      10  ; first result\n    (== n 1)  ; second test\n      ; second branch\n      20  ; second result\n    ; fallback\n    30))\n\n(fn (completePairs n)\n  (if (== n 0)\n      {\n        (let result 10)\n        result\n      }\n    (== n 1)\n      {\n        (let result 20)\n        result\n      }))\n"
    },
    {
      "name": "syntax-zoo",
      "input_sha256": "5fdf1a5e99208533eabc585369b111e67cd79e8f8ff678bd4797d36f18a2a2cc",
      "ok": 1,
      "text": "; Every declaration and expression form the parser accepts, in one file.\n;\n; `check-fmt.sh` formats every `.ax` file in the repository and re-runs\n; the suites against the result, which makes it exactly as wide as the\n; corpus - and the corpus uses `data`, `struct`, `fn` and `macro` and\n; nothing else. So the formatter was broken for most of the language and\n; the gate was green: `(type N Int)` lost its `=`, a `trait` lost its\n; `where`, its supertrait parentheses and its method `::`, and an `impl`\n; lost its `where` too. Each produced a file that no longer parsed, and\n; each was found by hand rather than by CI.\n;\n; This file exists so the gate stops depending on what the standard\n; library happens to need. It is never run - only parsed, formatted, and\n; parsed again - so it is written for coverage of *syntax*, not to\n; compute anything.\n(import Mem)\n(import Sys.Platform)\n; `Vec`, because the container `for` below reads its element through\n; `Vec::vecGet` and its bound through `Vec::vecLen`, resolved by\n; import path; with no path to `Vec` at all those two are AX3001 at the\n; loop, and this file is type-checked after it is formatted.\n(import Vec)\n\n; --- type alias -----------------------------------------------------\n(pub type Count Int)\n\n; --- data: positional, named fields, nullary ------------------------\n(pub data Shape\n  (Circle { r : Int })\n  (Rect { w : Int, h : Int })\n  (Empty))\n\n(data Tree a\n  (Leaf)\n  (Node (Tree a) a (Tree a)))\n\n; --- data: a deriving clause - REFUSED, 2026-08-14 -------------------\n; This zoo carried the repository's ONLY `deriving` clause, written\n; deliberately to pin the formatter's spelling of a clause the\n; compiler parsed and DISCARDED - no instance was ever derived from\n; it. MAC-CAP-9 settled on refusing the clause (`derive` is explicit\n; declaration macros), and macro-system.md counted this file pair as\n; the change's whole migration cost before the commit landed. The\n; refusal is AX2004 from the parser and a poison from the formatter -\n; the two grammars refuse together - pinned by\n; tests/fmt/parity/070-deriving-refused.axp and\n; tests/diagnostics/545-deriving-refused.axbad.\n; --- struct ---------------------------------------------------------\n; `mut` sits INSIDE the field's parentheses, before the name. The\n; formatter printed `(y : Int) mut`, which does not parse either, and\n; for the same reason nothing noticed: no struct in the repository has\n; a mutable field.\n(pub struct Rec\n  (n : Int)\n  (mut counter : Int)\n  (on : Bool)\n  (ratio : Float)\n  (label : String))\n\n; --- trait: supertraits, methods, defaults --------------------------\n; The tag inside the `where` list is here for the same reason the one\n; in the `impl` below is: a trait method can carry a `;@axiom:` tag\n; since 2026-08-25 - a DEFAULT body's effects are declared on the\n; method - and the printer had no arm for it. The atom failed the\n; loop's identifier test and reached `fpBad`, which refuses the whole\n; FILE. `no_refactor` again, and not `effect(io)`: the printer's path\n; is the same for any key, and a claim would couple this file to the\n; effect walk.\n; --- impl -----------------------------------------------------------\n; Every method the trait declares, because since 2026-08-10 an `impl`\n; is a real declaration rather than an inert node: its bodies are\n; type-checked and a missing method is AX3025. This file carried only\n; `show` while nothing looked.\n;\n; The AXTAG and the comment are both here because an impl member can\n; carry a `;@axiom:` tag since 2026-08-25, and the printer had no arm\n; for either. The key is `no_refactor` - metadata, never validated -\n; and not `effect(io)`, because this file is about the NORMAL FORM and\n; the printer's path is the same for any key. A claim would couple the\n; zoo to the effect walk: the first draft here wrote `effect(io)` over\n; `(lambda (x) x)`, which performs nothing, and AX3010 refused the\n; formatted zoo. The AX3010 semantics are pinned by the diagnostics\n; corpus, not here. The tag hit the loop's `FN_PAREN` demand and refused the\n; WHOLE FILE with \"formatter refusal\"; the comment stayed queued until\n; the module ended and was re-emitted after the impl, which the\n; round-trip check cannot catch because its text and count both\n; survive being moved. Both are normal form now, and this case is what\n; keeps them so.\n; --- macro ----------------------------------------------------------\n(macro (twice x) (+ x x))\n\n; --- signatures: uncurried arrows, higher order ---------------------\n(pub :: apply (-> (-> Int Int) Int Int))\n(pub fn (apply f x)\n  (f x))\n\n; --- every literal kind ---------------------------------------------\n(:: literals (-> Int Int))\n(fn (literals _x)\n  {\n    (let i 42)\n    (let f 1.5)\n    (let c 'q')\n    (let s \"tab\\there \\\"quoted\\\" back\\\\slash\")\n    (let b true)\n    i\n  })\n\n; --- control flow ---------------------------------------------------\n(:: control (-> Int Int))\n;@axiom:effect(pure)\n(fn (control n)\n  {\n    (let mut i 0)\n    (let mut acc 0)\n    {\n      (while (< i n)\n        {\n          (set acc (+ acc i))\n          (set i (+ i 1))\n        })\n      (if (> acc 10)\n        acc\n        0)\n    }\n  })\n\n; --- for, both shapes ----------------------------------------------\n(:: counted (-> (Vec Int) Int))\n(fn (counted xs)\n  {\n    (let mut acc 0)\n    {\n      (for i in 0..4\n        (set acc (+ acc i)))\n      (for x in xs\n        {\n          (set acc (+ acc x))\n          (set acc (+ acc 1))\n        })\n      acc\n    }\n  })\n\n; --- region --------------------------------------------------------\n(:: arenaSum (-> Int Int))\n(fn (arenaSum n)\n  (region r\n    {\n      (let mut acc 0)\n      {\n        (while (< acc n)\n          (set acc (+ acc 1)))\n        acc\n      }\n    }))\n\n; --- variadic if ---------------------------------------------------\n(:: classify (-> Int Int))\n(fn (classify n)\n  (if (< n 0)\n      1\n    (== n 0)\n      2\n    {\n      (+ n 0)\n      3\n    }))\n\n; --- pattern matching: nested, named fields, punning ----------------\n(:: area (-> Shape Int))\n(fn (area s)\n  (match s\n    ((Circle {r = r}) (* r r))\n    ((Rect {w = w, h = h}) (* w h))\n    ((Empty) 0)))\n\n(:: depth (-> (Tree Int) Int))\n(fn (depth t)\n  (match t\n    ((Leaf) 0)\n    ((Node l _v r) (+ 1 (depth l)))))\n\n; --- `_` HOLES: a bare wildcard PATTERN and an expression-position\n; PARTIAL-APPLICATION HOLE, the same token meaning two different\n; things by POSITION. Neither appeared in this file before: a bare\n; `_` pattern never printed here (`_v` above is a NAME that happens to\n; start with `_`, which binds and is a different thing), and\n; expression position had no meaning for `_` at all until holes -\n; `check` on a bare `(add2 1 _)` before this feature existed reported\n; `undefined variable `_``. `(add2 1 _)` desugars to a lambda before\n; the printer ever sees it, so this is the SOURCE spelling that must\n; round-trip, not what it expands to.\n(:: add2 (-> Int Int Int))\n(fn (add2 x y)\n  (+ x y))\n\n(:: ignoresIts (-> Int Int Int))\n(fn (ignoresIts _ y)\n  y)\n\n(:: holeExample Int)\n(fn (holeExample)\n  {\n    (let addOne (add2 1 _))\n    (addOne 41)\n  })\n\n; --- field access, casts, sizeof ------------------------------------\n(:: misc (-> Rec Int))\n(fn (misc r)\n  (+ (+ r.n (as r.ratio Int)) (+ (sizeof Int) (alignof Int))))\n\n; --- qualified names ------------------------------------------------\n(:: qualified (-> Addr Int))\n(fn (qualified x)\n  (Mem::addrHash x))\n\n; --- trailing comment on a declaration ------------------------------\n(:: last (-> Int Int))\n(fn (last x)\n  x)\n\n; stays on this line\n; --- effect declarations and handle expressions ---------------------\n(effect Console\n  (log :: (-> String Int)))\n\n(effect Wire\n  (send :: (-> Int Int Int))\n  (recv :: (-> Int Int)))\n\n(:: handled (-> Int Int))\n(fn (handled n)\n  (handle\n    (log \"hi\")\n    (Console)\n    (lambda (s) 0)))\n\n(:: scoped (-> Int Int))\n(fn (scoped n)\n  (handle\n    n\n    (IO Mut)\n    0))\n\n; --- a parenthesised type application, in every position that takes one\n;\n; `(Box Int)` is two tokens with no delimiter of its own, so a position\n; that prints it bare hands its parent an extra argument. The forms\n; below are the sixteen places the grammar allows one, and the\n; formatter printed fourteen of them bare - `(:: b (Box Int))` came out\n; as `(:: b Box Int)`, which does not even parse. The two that survived\n; did so by accident: `forall` and a type signature *expression* both\n; re-read the juxtaposition as the same application.\n;\n; None of it was visible, because every type in this repository is\n; either a bare name or an arrow, and an arrow's components were the\n; one position that already parenthesised. That is this file's whole\n; job: the corpus does not ask, so the zoo has to.\n(pub data Box a\n  (B a))\n\n(:: aBox (Box Int))\n(fn (aBox)\n  (B 1))\n\n(pub type BoxedCount (Box Int))\n\n(struct ZooCap (a)\n  (render : (-> a String)))\n\n(struct ZooPair (a b)\n  (fst : a)\n  (snd : b))\n\n(struct Holder\n  (item : (Box Int)))\n\n(data Wrapped\n  (W { inner : (Box Int) }))\n\n; An effect operation's printed type is its parameters arrowed onto its\n; return type, so it reaches the bare position only when it has no\n; parameters - and `AX3017` refuses one of those (\"an operation needs at\n; least one to be callable\"). The bare spelling is therefore\n; unreachable from a program the checker accepts, and this declaration\n; covers the position that is reachable.\n(effect Boxes\n  (store :: (-> (Box Int) Int)))\n\n; The type-form interiors: each of these brings its own delimiters, so\n; the application inside it is the bare position under test. They ride\n; on an arrow's argument so that no definition is owed for them.\n;\n; `(forall a T)`, the tuple `(A B)` and the list `[T]` are absent\n; deliberately. The formatter prints a `TForall`, but `parse_type`\n; has no `forall` branch, so `(forall a a)` falls into the refused\n; tuple position (AX2004) - and a real tuple or list interior cannot\n; go in a file the gates must accept, because the parser refuses\n; both (`tests/diagnostics/944-removed-list-type.axbad`,\n; `946-removed-tuple-type.axbad`).\n(:: interiors (-> (* (Box Int)) (Box Int) Int))\n(fn (interiors p n)\n  0)\n\n(:: typed_exprs (-> Int Int))\n;@axiom:effect(unsafe)\n(fn (typed_exprs n)\n  {\n    (let s (:: (B 1) (Box Int)))\n    (let c (as 0 (Box Int)))\n    (+ (sizeof (Box Int)) (alignof (Box Int)))\n  })\n\n; --- the normalisations: what `fmt` REWRITES rather than reprints ----\n;\n; Everything above asks whether a construct survives formatting. These\n; ask the other question: which spellings the formatter deliberately\n; replaces. Each line below comes out as something else, and\n; `tests/fmt/syntax-zoo.expected.ax` is what it comes out as - so the\n; normal form is a reviewed artefact rather than whatever the printer\n; happens to do this week.\n;\n; The list is exhaustive as of the day it was written, one entry per\n; measured rewrite:\n;\n;   1_000_000                -> 1000000\n;   007                      -> 7\n;   ((g 1) 2)                -> (g 1 2)\n;   (e)                      -> e\n;   { e }                    -> e\n;   two body expressions     -> a { } block\n;   (-> Int (-> Int Int))    -> (-> Int Int Int)\n;   (Int)                    -> Int\n;   ()  as a type            -> ()\n;   Q.Inner::only            -> Q::Inner::only\n;\n; plus, not visible as a single line: imports hoisted above every other\n; declaration, exactly one blank line between declarations and none kept\n; from the source, and string and char literals re-escaped from their\n; decoded values.\n;\n; A float is printed as the shortest decimal that round-trips its `f64`.\n; Every float literal in this repository is an exact short binary\n; fraction, so stripping trailing zeros after the point (keeping one) is\n; the same function today - a property of this corpus, not of the\n; printer. A float needing seventeen significant digits would break that\n; equivalence, which is why one is not written here.\n(:: helperD (-> Int Int))\n(fn (helperD x)\n  x)\n\n(:: zeroParam Int)\n(fn (zeroParam)\n  7)\n\n(:: bigNums Int)\n(fn (bigNums)\n  (+ 1000000 7))\n\n(:: addTwo (-> Int Int Int))\n(fn (addTwo a b)\n  (+ a b))\n\n(:: spine Int)\n(fn (spine)\n  (addTwo 1 2))\n\n(:: redundantParens (-> Int Int))\n(fn (redundantParens n)\n  (n))\n\n(:: oneBlock (-> Int Int))\n(fn (oneBlock n)\n  n)\n\n(:: manyBody (-> Int Int))\n(fn (manyBody n)\n  {\n    1\n    n\n  })\n\n(:: curried (-> Int Int Int))\n(fn (curried a b)\n  a)\n\n; `(Int)` -> `Int` is measured and real, but it cannot live here: the\n; editor grammar has no parenthesised-type rule, deliberately - adding\n; one made it ambiguous against a type-parameter list and against a\n; zero-argument application at once - so a file both gates must accept\n; cannot contain one.\n(:: unitArg (-> () Int))\n(fn (unitArg n)\n  1)\n\n; Negative literals, in the four positions that read differently: a\n; binding, an argument, after a leading operand, and the space-separated\n; spelling that stays unary minus and prints back as a literal.\n(:: negatives Int)\n(fn (negatives)\n  {\n    (let a -5)\n    (let b (+ 3 -1))\n    (let c (- 0 -4))\n    (let d -5)\n    (+ a (+ b (+ c d)))\n  })\n\n; --- struct: the same shape, generated by a declaration macro -------\n; `RecTwin` is `Rec` written by a template, field for field. Its AXSYM\n; row must carry `Rec`'s own `#fields=` - the expander built each\n; generated field from the parser's float-flag word instead of its\n; type node, so the row read `n:{unknown},...` and a bare `Float` field\n; crashed expansion outright (fixed 2026-08-28, pinned at runtime by\n; tests/selfhost/396-macro-struct-field-types.ax). The two rows in\n; tests/tools/symbols-zoo.golden are the compile-time half.\n(macro defRecTwin ((defRecTwin N)\n   (struct N\n     (n : Int)\n     (mut counter : Int)\n     (on : Bool)\n     (ratio : Float)\n     (label : String))))\n\n(defRecTwin RecTwin)\n\n; --- AXTAGs split across the two halves of one function -------------\n; A tag attaches to the declaration it is written above, and a function\n; has two. `symbols` renders the UNION of both sets on the one row, in\n; source order - `#no_refactor #pure` for `splitTags`. It rendered the\n; last non-empty set alone until 2026-08-29 (`symbols.ax`, `smTags`):\n; `#pure` here, and `#no_refactor` with the two tags swapped.\n;@axiom:no_refactor\n(:: splitTags (-> Int Int))\n;@axiom:effect(pure)\n(fn (splitTags n)\n  (+ n 1))\n\n; --- parallel: the concurrency form, both binding-list shapes ------\n; Spawns every binding beside the caller and joins them in argument\n; order; the value is the body's (`core/parser.ax`'s\n; `parseParallelExpr`). `IO`, because a spawn reaches the kernel.\n(:: beside (-> Int Int))\n;@axiom:effect(io)\n;@axiom:effect(spawn)\n;@axiom:effect(block)\n(fn (beside n)\n  (parallel p (\n    (a (+ n 1))\n    (b (+ n 2))\n  )\n    (+ a b)))\n\n(:: alone (-> Int Int))\n;@axiom:effect(io)\n;@axiom:effect(spawn)\n;@axiom:effect(block)\n(fn (alone n)\n  (parallel p ((a (* n 2)))\n    {\n      (+ a 1)\n      a\n    }))\n\n; --- a restriction, rendered on the AXSYM row ------------------------\n; `restrict(...)` takes a comma-separated list; `symbols` renders it as\n; `#restrict=no-io,no-cast`, the value byte for byte, before the\n; compiler's own derived keys - so a policy gate can read the claim and\n; the checker's row off one line (`scripts/check-restrictions.sh`).\n;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-cast)\n(:: restrictedTwice (-> Int Int))\n(fn (restrictedTwice n)\n  (* n 3))\n\n; --- ordinary types carry inferred reference origins ---\n(:: regionKeep (-> (Vec String) String a Int))\n(fn (regionKeep t s x)\n  7)\n"
    }
  ]
}
